window.docContents[33].resolve({"/Appendix/#section-chapter-12-algebraic-graph-theory":{"contents":"* Iso.autCongr: transport of the automorphism group of a graph along an\nisomorphism.* cycleGraphReflect: automorphism of C[n] reflecting the vertices (k ↦ -k).* autCycleGraphMulEquivDihedral: the automorphism group of C[n + 3] is isomorphic\nto DihedralGroup (n + 3).* autHypercubeTwoMulEquivDihedral: the automorphism group of Q[2] is isomorphic\nto DihedralGroup 4.* mycielskianLift: the vector on the vertices of mμ[G] with value 0 at the\napex, t * x v at v ₗ and x v at v ᵣ.* HypercubeVert.equivPi: equivalence between the hypercube vertex types\nHypercubeVert n and Fin n → Bool, via Fin.snoc.* hypercubeIsoHamming: the hypercube Q[n] is isomorphic to the Hamming cube\nhypercubeHamming n.* hammingTranslate / hammingPermute: translation by a fixed vector (pointwise\nxor) and coordinate permutation as automorphisms of the Hamming cube.* hammingPhi: the action of Equiv.Perm (Fin n) on translation vectors by\npermuting coordinates.* hyperoctahedralGroup: the hyperoctahedral group (ℤ/2)ⁿ ⋊ Sₙ, i.e. the wreath\nproduct ℤ/2 ≀ Sₙ, as a semidirect product.* hammingTranslateHom / hammingPermuteHom: translations and coordinate\npermutations as monoid homs into the automorphism group of the Hamming cube.* hyperoctahedralToAut: the homomorphism from the hyperoctahedral group to the\nautomorphisms of the Hamming cube.* hyperoctahedralMulEquivAutHamming: the automorphism group of the Hamming cube is\nthe hyperoctahedral group.* autHypercubeMulEquivHyperoctahedral: the automorphism group of Q[n] is the\nhyperoctahedral group ℤ/2 ≀ Sₙ.* chosenSubgraphIso: a choice, for each subgraph C of B isomorphic to A, of a\nreference isomorphism A ≃g C.coe.* subgraphIsoEquivAut: equivalence between the isomorphisms A ≃g C.coe of a\nsubgraph C isomorphic to A and the automorphisms of A.* copyEquivSigmaIso: equivalence between A.Copy B and the Sigma type of subgraphs\nof B isomorphic to A with their isomorphisms.* copyEquivSubgraphIsoProdAut: equivalence\nA.Copy B ≃ {C : B.Subgraph | Nonempty (A ≃g C.coe)} × (A ≃g A).* Iso.copyEquivIso: for finite A, an isomorphism A ≃g B identifies copies of\nA in B with isomorphisms A ≃g B.* copyEquivAut: copies of a finite graph in itself are equivalent to its\nautomorphisms.* copyEquivSubgraphIsoProdCopy: A.Copy B is equivalent to the subgraphs of B\nisomorphic to A paired with copies of A in itself.* Iso.copyCongr: isomorphisms A ≃g A' and B ≃g B' identify the copies of A\nin B with the copies of A' in B'.* extendEmb / extendHom: extension of a k-vector to an n-vector along an\nembedding of directions Fin k ↪ Fin n, and the induced homomorphism of Hamming\ncubes.* subcubeCopy: the canonical copy of Q[k] in Q[n] with a given base point and\nset of free directions.* copyEquivProd: the copies of Q[k] in Q[n] correspond to pairs of a base point\nand a direction embedding.* copyEquivEmbedding: if every copy of A in B reflects adjacency, then copies\nof A in B are the same as embeddings A ↪g B.\n\nFrom the exercises:\n\n* autMulLeftCayley: left multiplication by g as an automorphism of the Cayley\ngraph mulCayley s.* autTopMulEquivPerm: the automorphism group of a complete graph is the symmetric\ngroup.* autComplMulEquiv: a graph and its complement have the same automorphism group.* boolVecEquivFinset: Bool vectors of length n correspond to subsets of Fin n.* hypercubeHammingIsoHasse: the Hamming cube is isomorphic to the Hasse diagram\nhypercubeHasse n.* copyTopEquiv: copies of a graph in a complete graph are the injective maps of\nvertices.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Appendix\u0009Definitions","header":"14.2.11. Chapter 12: Algebraic Graph Theory","id":"/Appendix/#section-chapter-12-algebraic-graph-theory"},"/Constructions/#section-operations-on-graphs":{"contents":"So far each construction has built a graph from scratch. We now look at some ways\nof building new graphs from old ones.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Constructions","header":"3.3. Operations on Graphs","id":"/Constructions/#section-operations-on-graphs"},"/SimpleGraph/#section-sets-as-types":{"contents":"Although G.neighborSet u is a Set V, Lean also lets us use it as a type. A term\nw : G.neighborSet u is a vertex together with a proof that this vertex is a\nneighbour of u.\n\nLean displays this type as ↑(G.neighborSet u): it is the subtype\n{w : V // w ∈ G.neighborSet u}. We build such terms with the anonymous constructor\n⟨v, h⟩ and take them apart with .val (the vertex) and .property (the proof).\nLean inserts .val automatically wherever a term of V is expected, and displays it\nas ↑w.\n\nexample {u v : V} (h : G.Adj u v) : G.neighborSet u := ⟨v, h⟩\n\n\nexample {u : V} (w : G.neighborSet u) : G.Adj u w.val := by\n  exact w.property\n\n\nexample {u : V} (w : G.neighborSet u) : G.Adj u w := by\n  exact w.property\n\n\nsection darts\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009SimpleGraph\u0009Vertices\u0009Neighbours","header":"2.3.2.1. Sets as Types","id":"/SimpleGraph/#section-sets-as-types"},"/Subgraphs/#section-subgraphs":{"contents":"Given G : SimpleGraph V, G.Subgraph is the type of general subgraphs of G.\n\n#source Subgraph\n\n\nIf H : G.Subgraph then we have the following data:\n\n#check H.verts      -- Set V the vertex set of the subgraph H\n#check H.Adj        -- V → V → Prop the adjacency relation of the subgraph H\n#check H.adj_sub    -- H.Adj v w → G.Adj v w (i.e. E(H) ⊆ E(G))\n#check H.edge_vert  -- H.Adj v w → v ∈ H.verts edges of H only join verts of H\n#check H.symm       -- Std.Symm H.Adj: H.Adj v w → H.Adj w v (unoriented edges)\n\n\nexample (H : G.Subgraph) (v w : V) (h : H.Adj v w) : H.Adj w v := h.symm\n\n\nIn order to prove that two subgraphs H₁ and H₂ of G : SimpleGraph V are equal\nwe need to show that they have the same vertex set and the same edges. We can do this\nusing ext as we did for SimpleGraph V (Subgraph also has the @[ext] attribute\non its definition) but now this will give us two goals: one for equality of vertex\nsets and one for equality of adjacency relations (edges).\n\nexample (H₁ H₂ : G.Subgraph) (h₁ : H₁.verts = H₂.verts)\n    (h₂ : ∀ v w, H₁.Adj v w ↔ H₂.Adj v w) : H₁ = H₂ := by\n  ext v w\n  · rw [h₁]\n  · apply h₂\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Subgraphs","header":"9.1. Subgraphs","id":"/Subgraphs/#section-subgraphs"}});